// throttling.kql
// Query error rate (429 + 503) grouped by 5-minute window.
//
// Azure AI Search returns TWO distinct error codes under load:
//   429 — QPS rate limit exceeded (queries-per-second cap for the service tier)
//   503 — Service overloaded (CPU/memory exhaustion or queue overflow)
//
// The built-in "Throttled search queries percentage" metric in Azure Monitor counts
// BOTH 429 and 503 as throttled. Querying only for 429 will return zero rows when
// the service is saturated via resource exhaustion (the more common case on large indexes).
//
// Columns: TimeGenerated, total, errors_429, errors_503, throttled, throttle_pct,
//          est_unique_503s
//
// *** PREREQUISITE — Log Analytics workspace, NOT Storage Account ***
//   This query uses the AzureDiagnostics table which is ONLY populated when the
//   diagnostic setting destination is a Log Analytics workspace.
//   Routing logs to a Storage Account does NOT populate AzureDiagnostics —
//   the query will fail or return zero rows.
//
//   To add a Log Analytics destination (existing Storage routing can stay):
//     Portal → Search service → Monitoring → Diagnostic settings → Add diagnostic setting
//       ✓ OperationLogs
//       ✓ Send to Log Analytics workspace  ← must be checked
//     Allow 5–10 minutes after enabling for the first rows to appear.
//
// Note: AzureDiagnostics uses dynamic column typing — numeric HTTP status codes land in
// resultSignature_d (double) and string values in resultSignature_s. column_ifexists() is used
// so the query does not fail on workspaces that have never seen these codes (columns absent).
//
// *** RETRY AMPLIFICATION — why errors_503 overstates application failures ***
//   The Azure SDK default retry policy retries 503s automatically (retry_total=3).
//   Each application-level failed call fires up to 4 HTTP requests, all of which
//   appear as separate 503 rows in AzureDiagnostics. This means:
//
//     errors_503 (KQL) ≈ application failures × retry_factor
//
//   est_unique_503s divides by retry_factor to estimate distinct application-level
//   failures (lower bound — assumes all retries also failed).
//   Retrying a saturated node adds load and worsens 503s for other clients.
//   See retry-amplification.kql to detect retry bursts per client IP.

AzureDiagnostics
| where TimeGenerated > ago(1h)            // narrow scan first — AzureDiagnostics is huge
| where ResourceProvider == "MICROSOFT.SEARCH"
| where OperationName startswith "Query."
| extend
    sig_d = todouble(column_ifexists("resultSignature_d", real(null))),
    sig_s = tostring(column_ifexists("resultSignature_s", ""))
| summarize
    total      = count(),
    errors_429 = countif(sig_d == 429 or sig_s == "429"),
    errors_503 = countif(sig_d == 503 or sig_s == "503")
    by bin(TimeGenerated, 5m)
| extend
    throttled        = errors_429 + errors_503,
    throttle_pct     = round(todouble(errors_429 + errors_503) / todouble(total) * 100, 2),
    // Lower bound on distinct application-level 503 failures (assumes all retries also failed).
    // Divisor = retry_total + 1 = 4 (SDK default). Change to 1.0 if retries are disabled.
    est_unique_503s  = round(todouble(errors_503) / 4.0, 0)
| project TimeGenerated, total, errors_429, errors_503, est_unique_503s, throttled, throttle_pct
| order by TimeGenerated asc
